  "notes": "The producer-mixer of the late-2000s alt-rock/pop-punk crossover — loud, gridded, radio-first: 82–171 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 and the singles at 86–138, which is where `bpm.mode` sits. The governing fact is that **he produces and mixes the same record, so the drum tone is designed for the mix**, and the kit is \"edited hard to the grid and mixed very loud\" — hence a `quantizeStrength` of 0.97, the hardest here, against the loudest accent tier, and a very tight velocity variance. Three deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and the '&' of 2\", \"short bright snare on 2&4\", and \"one-bar 16th tom fills into choruses\" plus \"breakdowns dropping to kick-and-clap\", which is the tom perc block, a 4-bar fill schedule and a high `dropOutBeats`. He later commercialised his drum samples and signature processing as products, \"which is a fair description of how consistent his kit sound is\" — the reason the velocity tiers here are narrow rather than spread. Harmony is flat-side minors over i–VI–III–VII and i–VII–VI with \"power-chord voicings over an open-string drone, so the vocal decides the modality\", which is the high triad share and the open voicing. The identity is \"a syncopated palm-muted riff\" with guitars \"stacked in tight doubles rather than wide layers\", and the vocal is compressed forward with the belt at the top of the singer's range. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says the drum detail is inferred. ⚠ Every drum probability here is **INFERRED**.",
